{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794574609,"nonce":"A4G1SywjvjiVHId2WvidfCgysV0=","message":"Tuple bindings now use hxn.Tuple\u003cA, B, ...\u003e, as requested. Tuple.of(\"name\", 42) infers the position types, while new hxn.Tuple\u003cString, Int\u003e(\"name\", 42) supplies them explicitly. Numbered record literals remain accepted through struct initialization. Positions _0, _1, etc. remain mutable and typed, consistent with Haxe data records; length is fixed. copy returns a shallow copy and toArray a separate Array\u003cDynamic\u003e for inspecting mixed values. The existing Rust encoding and manifest names are unchanged.\n\nThe generic-build macro defines one ordinary generic Haxe class per arity, under stable hxn.tuple.TupleN names. This keeps the public Tuple type variadic without maintaining a fixed family of handwritten wrappers, and makes the same classes available in CPPIA hosts and their scripts. The zero-parameter namespace resolves the Tuple.of macro; an empty tuple constructor is rejected. The CLI embeds both Tuple.hx and TupleMacro.hx into every generated binding and package. The generator returns actual tuple objects after reading each element into a local in position order. No runtime support crate or native ABI changed.\n\nRuntime checks cover inferred and explicit constructors, one-element and five-element tuples, nesting, expected-type inference with an empty array, copying and editing the copy, independent inspection arrays, and preservation of i64 precision through both helpers. The existing tuple fixture still checks records, collections, optional elements, futures, streams, signals, async functions, callbacks, and failures. The pantry example formats returned name/quantity tuples with toArray. The CLI test compiles and runs Tuple.of using only the package, then checks compile-time rejection of wrong position types, wrong arity, a wrongly typed field assignment, and an empty tuple.\n\nValidation, with runtime tests under timeouts:\n- macOS arm64: workspace tests, formatting, strict Clippy, and shell syntax checks pass. All nine counter modes pass. CLI and examples pass on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm and Emscripten; C imports pass in all supported modes, including native SQLite where supported. Generated native Ash modes, hxcpp and CPPIA pass three repetitions each, and generated wasm/Emscripten pass twice after their initial runs. The CPPIA fixture also creates a tuple arity not used by the library API, checking classes created only in the script.\n- Linux x86-64 through ssh nuc: workspace tests, strict Clippy, stock HashLink fixtures, generated Ash/hxcpp/CPPIA fixtures, and all native CLI/example/C-import modes pass. The generated program passes three repetitions each on stock HashLink and Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter. SHA-256 verification includes both new Haxe support files and every tracked local file; Haxe/hxcpp configuration is confined to the temporary task copy.\n- Mobile: the full example suite passes twice each on the iPhone 15 iOS 17.5 simulator through iphonesim and the Pixel 9 Pro Android emulator through ADB. Both devices were shut down afterwards.\n\nNot run: Windows (awaiting a push and CI), Linux wasm/Emscripten (both paths ran on macOS), or a physical mobile device. The optional NUC SQLite import is skipped because sqlite3.h is absent. Miri was not rerun because hxn-abi and hxn-future did not change.\n\nThe wrapper change is uncommitted. The broader generator issue remains open for its other gaps.","files":null}]}